  <p>
    The melanocortin system is best known for pigment, the stress axis, appetite, and
    inflammation. Sexual function is its quietest and most surprising reach — and it
    arrives by the same route as everything else the system does in the head: through{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R</a>, the appetite receptor, acting centrally in the
    brain.<Cite n={1} /> The remarkable part is not that a melanocortin drug can improve
    the mechanics of sex. It is that one can lift <em>desire</em> itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Desire is upstream of plumbing</h2>
  <p>
    The cleanest way to understand melanocortin sexual pharmacology is to separate two
    things that everyday language runs together: the <strong>wanting</strong> and the
    <strong>mechanics</strong>. PDE5 inhibitors — Viagra and its relatives — work on the
    mechanics. They act in the periphery, improving blood flow in the genitals, and they
    do so only once arousal is already present; they carry out an order the brain has
    already given. A melanocortin agonist works at the other end. It acts centrally, on
    the circuits that generate desire and arousal in the first place, so it can start the
    signal rather than merely execute it.<Cite n={1} /><Cite n={3} />
  </p>

  <Figure
    caption="Two drugs, two points in the same act. A melanocortin agonist acts centrally, on MC4R circuits that generate desire — the signal. A PDE5 inhibitor acts peripherally on blood flow — the plumbing, and only once arousal is already present. One starts the order; the other carries it out."
    source="Central vs. peripheral mechanism per Molinoff et al., Ann N Y Acad Sci 2003 (PMID 12851303) and the VYLEESI prescribing information. Schematic."
  >
    <DesireNotPlumbing />
  </Figure>

  <h2>Found, not designed</h2>
  <p>
    No one set out to build a desire drug from the melanocortin system. The branch opened
    by accident. In trials of <a href="/melanotan">Melanotan II</a>, the peptide being
    pushed as a sunless tan, researchers kept noting a striking and consistent side
    effect: spontaneous sexual arousal. Rather than discard it, chemists re-worked the
    molecule to strip out most of the pigment activity while keeping the pro-sexual
    effect — and the result became <a href="/bremelanotide">PT-141 / bremelanotide</a>.<Cite n={1} />
    It is the same lineage that produced the approved tanning drug{' '}
    <a href="/afamelanotide">afamelanotide</a>; where afamelanotide is the pigment side of
    the melanotan story made into medicine, bremelanotide is the other side effect made
    into one. For how tangled that family tree gets, see{' '}
    <a href="/pt-141-vs-melanotan">PT-141 vs Melanotan</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What the evidence actually shows</h2>
  <p>
    The central effect is not just a mechanistic story; it has been tested in both sexes.
    In men, an intranasal melanocortin agonist produced erections in healthy volunteers
    and in patients with mild-to-moderate erectile dysfunction, acting through the brain
    rather than the bloodstream — a different route from the PDE5 inhibitors, and one that
    worked in some men they did not.<Cite n={2} /> In women, the pivotal RECONNECT trials
    tested bremelanotide for acquired, generalised <strong>hypoactive sexual desire
    disorder (HSDD)</strong> — a persistent, distressing lack of sexual desire — and showed
    improvements in desire and in the distress it causes.<Cite n={4} /> On that basis the
    FDA approved it in 2019, the first on-demand treatment for HSDD in premenopausal
    women.<Cite n={3} />
  </p>
  <p>
    The two stories end differently, and that difference is itself informative. The
    programme in men was not carried to approval: an earlier intranasal route raised blood
    pressure enough to halt it, and development refocused on women with a subcutaneous
    dose.<Cite n={1} /> So the one approved melanocortin sexual-function drug treats desire
    in women, while the effect in men — real in early trials — remains off-label and
    unapproved.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest limits</h2>
  <p>
    This is a genuine effect, but a bounded one. Bremelanotide is approved for a specific
    diagnosis in a specific population, not as a general libido enhancer, and its
    tolerability is real: nausea is common, and because it transiently raises blood
    pressure it is contraindicated in uncontrolled hypertension and known cardiovascular
    disease.<Cite n={3} /> The grey-market “PT-141” sold as vials and nasal sprays is the
    unapproved version of the same molecule, of unknown purity and dose — the sexual-function
    counterpart to the <a href="/melanotan">melanotan</a> tanning peptides, and carrying the
    same caution. The science here is sound and specific; the marketing around it usually
    is not.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Sexual function is the melanocortin system doing, one more time, the thing this whole
    site keeps finding: reaching into a place no one expected, through a receptor it was
    already using for something else. The appetite switch turned out to sit in the circuitry
    of desire, and a tanning peptide's side effect turned out to be a drug. For the molecule,
    see <a href="/bremelanotide">bremelanotide (PT-141)</a>; for the receptor it works
    through, see <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R — the appetite rheostat</a>; for the lineage
    it came from, see <a href="/melanotan">Melanotan</a>.
  </p>

